{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794146475,"nonce":"vNTKiCZGyHRr9PaEyRvKeDgut0k=","title":"Emoji sequences draw as the base emoji plus a blank: a skin tone leaves a gap, a flag vanishes","message":"Reported on an iPhone 12 Pro Max and a Pixel 10 Pro, 2026-11-08: after typing `你好👋🏾hello` into an input the value was right (logged: 4F60 597D 1F44B 1F3FE 68 65 6C 6C 6F) but the screen showed the plain yellow hand, then a character of empty space, then \"hello\". Reproduced on the desktop in the cn input.\n\nCause. The primary face has no emoji glyphs, so it shapes a sequence into a glyph per code point, all in one cluster, each reporting the cluster's first code point. The renderer's cluster-duplicate rule skipped drawing the second glyph, but its advance (one em) stayed. The per-code-point emoji fallback never asked the emoji face for the sequence as a unit, so the face's skin-tone, ZWJ and flag glyphs were not used. A ZWJ family drew only its first member plus blanks; a flag drew nothing.\n\nFix. `FallbackFaces::apply_to(text, ..)` shapes each such cluster with the emoji face and keeps the single glyph it returns; the other code points become `FaceChoice::Joined` (no advance, no drawing); letter spacing is applied once per sequence. A sequence the face does not combine keeps today's behaviour. Tests: tests/fallback_layout.rs in blinc_text (width, one drawn glyph, toned glyph differs) and cn_input_caret.rs in blinc_recorder (the input holds the same width as one emoji and keeps the caret at the text end). Apple Color Emoji and Noto Color Emoji both return one glyph for skin tones and ZWJ families.\n\nNot covered: keycap sequences (`1️⃣`) and tag sequences; Android flags (separate issue).","files":null}]}